  "node_type": "statement",
  "body": {
    "id": "thm:unique-bijective-reduction",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "On a strictly positive finite-support chart on which each arm matrix \\(B_a\\) is square and nonsingular, \\(T_a\\) and \\(T_a^*\\) are bijective, \\(\\mathcal N_a(P)=\\mathcal K_a(P)=\\{0\\}\\), \\(\\mathcal C_P=\\{0\\}\\), and \\(\\Phi_P=\\{\\phi_{h,q}\\}\\).  The chart is locally unrestricted, and its canonical gradient is \\(\\phi_{\\mathrm{eff}}=\\phi_{h,q}\\).  Thus the quotient construction reduces exactly to the ordinary unique-bridge score on this finite bijective endpoint.",
    "depends_on": [
      "def:score-family",
      "def:normal-space",
      "def:affine-score-hull",
      "def:quotient-gradient",
      "lem:normal-affine-identity"
    ],
    "justification": "The reduction is the standard-case sanity check once the general score-lifting obligation is discharged.",
    "gap": "CuiEtAl2024SemiparametricProximal establishes the unique-and-surjective endpoint; this theorem verifies exact nesting without imposing those restrictions on the main model.",
    "consumer": "Existing proximal software remains efficiency-valid when diagnostics support unique bijective bridge operators.",
    "proof_tex": "For finite equal-cardinality proxy supports, the conditional operator and its adjoint are represented, after the positive diagonal changes induced by the Hilbert-space weights, by \\(B_a\\) and \\(B_a^\\top\\).  Nonsingularity therefore makes both operators bijective.  Hence their kernels are zero, the outcome and treatment bridge solutions are unique, and every \\(F,G,R\\) generator in \\(\\mathrm{def:normal-space}\\) is zero.  It follows that \\(\\mathcal C_P=\\{0\\}\\) and, by uniqueness, \\(\\Phi_P=\\{\\phi_{h,q}\\}\\).\n\nNonsingularity is open.  On a sufficiently small strictly positive neighborhood it persists, and both bridge equations are automatically solvable, so the local joint model imposes no equality constraint.  Every mean-zero finite-cell score is realized by \\(p_t=p(1+ts)\\) for sufficiently small \\(|t|\\); positivity and bounded likelihood ratios hold because the support is finite.  Thus \\(\\mathcal T_P=L_0^2(P)\\).  The finite derivative calculation\n\\[\n \\dot\\mu_a=\\dot d^\\top h_a+q_a^\\top(\\dot b_a-\\dot B_ah_a)=E[S_a(h_a,q_a)s]\n\\]\nshows that \\(\\phi_{h,q}\\) is a gradient.  Since the tangent space is all of \\(L_0^2(P)\\), that gradient is unique and is the canonical gradient.  Because \\(\\mathcal A_P\\) is the singleton containing it, its minimum-norm element is the same function, completing the reduction.",
